Overtone Fog

Ingredients: TUVAN-INSPIRED OVERTONE SINGING + SHOEGAZE + BOWED ELECTRIC GUITAR + HARMONIUM + FLOOR TOM + ANALOG TAPE

Creative Catalyst: The genre began during a winter residency in an abandoned radio observatory in northern Finland, where two overtone singers were testing sustained khöömei- and kargyraa-inspired techniques against the long metallic reflections of a decommissioned tracking dome. A shoegaze guitarist in the same residency discovered that heavily amplified, slowly bowed guitar did not obscure the voices if distortion was tuned around the singers’ moving overtone peaks instead of spread across the entire spectrum. Harmonium, floor tom, and tape loops were added later, producing a sound in which voices and amplification behave like one weather system rather than separate lead and backing layers.

Overtone Fog is a dense but slow-moving vocal-amplifier form where overtone singing supplies spectral motion and shoegaze supplies mass, sustain, and harmonic blur. The central idea is not contrast between “traditional voice” and “electric guitar.” The two are fused into one continuously changing field. A low vocal fundamental may remain nearly fixed while the singer reshapes mouth cavity and tongue position to sweep a bright overtone through the spectrum; the guitar responds by opening or closing narrow distortion bands around that moving partial, so the wall of sound seems to breathe from inside.

The primary voices alternate between low kargyraa-like growl, focused khöömei-like midrange, whistle-bright overtone peaks, unison drones, and slowly beating intervals. The singers are recorded close enough to preserve chest resonance, breath rasp, saliva clicks, and mouth-cavity changes, but the production avoids hyper-clean isolation. A distant dome microphone captures the long return so the listener hears the direct throat tone and the delayed architectural bloom as two related layers.

Bowed electric guitar is the principal amplified instrument. E-bow, violin bow, feedback, volume swells, reverse reverb, and slow tremolo produce sustained clouds instead of strummed chord sheets. Distortion is multiband: low-mid body can remain warm and compressed while upper bands are dynamically carved whenever a vocal overtone becomes prominent. Conventional shoegaze wash is therefore reorganized around live spectral movement rather than static pedal chains.

Harmonium supplies a narrow acoustic pressure floor. It holds one or two reed registers at a time, mostly tonic, fifth, suspended second, or modal upper tones. Bellows noise and reed instability are retained. The harmonium is not used for nostalgic or devotional color; it acts as a stable acoustic reference beneath voices whose overtone emphasis is constantly changing.

Percussion is deliberately primitive and infrequent. Floor tom, low kick, soft mallet cymbal, and boot-stamp impacts appear as weather fronts rather than grooves. A single tom hit may open a new feedback band, while several bars can pass without percussion. When rhythm becomes denser, it comes from repeated breath pulses, throat attacks, tremolo-guitar modulation, and tape-loop flutter as much as from drums.

Analog tape is the memory layer. Short vocal drones, amplifier hum, harmonium reed noise, and dome reflections are captured onto loops that slowly lose high-frequency detail. These loops are never synchronized perfectly. Their drift produces long-phase interference against the live singers and makes the background seem to change even when no new note is added.

The defining tension is body versus atmosphere: every sound begins as something physically made by lungs, strings, reeds, membranes, or magnetic tape, yet sustained resonance merges those sources until the listener can no longer identify where voice ends and amplifier begins.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 54–74 BPM, usually 4/4 or free-meter openings that later settle into a very slow pulse. Percussion is sparse; perceived motion often comes from breath cycles, tremolo rates, feedback swell, and tape drift rather than a constant beat.
- Harmonic Palette: Dorian, Aeolian, open fifths, suspended seconds, minor seconds, neutral intervals, harmonic-series relationships, low pedal tones, and deliberately slow beating between near-unisons. Chord changes are infrequent; spectral redistribution matters more than functional progression.
- Production Signatures:
  1. Overtone Notch Bloom — a moving narrow EQ notch follows the singer’s strongest overtone inside the guitar wall, creating a momentary pocket of air around each spectral peak.
  2. Dome Double — close vocal microphones are paired with a distant architectural return, but the room signal is allowed to arrive naturally late rather than time-aligned.
  3. Bow-Cloud Compression — bowed guitar feedback is compressed in slow multiband stages so new harmonics emerge gradually instead of exploding into a flat wall.
  4. Tape Snow Drift — unsynchronized loops of breath, harmonium reed noise, and amplifier hum run slightly off-speed, producing long-form phasing and soft high-frequency erosion.
  5. Throat-Tom Transfer — selected low kargyraa-like attacks are answered by one floor-tom strike tuned near the vocal fundamental, briefly transferring low-frequency weight from chest resonance to membrane.
- Dynamics: Intensity grows through thicker overtone stacking, wider guitar feedback bands, stronger vocal distortion, denser tape accumulation, and shorter silence between low percussion events. Peak sections remain slow and can feel enormous without becoming rhythmically busy.

Scene & Origin Lore

Overtone Fog formed during an experimental winter residency inside a decommissioned radio observatory near Sodankylä. The main tracking dome had lost its antenna hardware but retained curved steel ribs, cable trenches, faded coordinate markings, maintenance ladders, equipment lockers, perforated acoustic panels, and a circular floor whose reflections changed dramatically depending on where a performer stood.

Vocalists Anu Saarinen and Temir Oyun had been developing non-lyrical overtone studies designed around sustained fundamentals and moving partials. Guitarist Leena Koskinen was working separately on a bowed-feedback project and noticed that one particular amplifier position made her upper harmonics disappear whenever Temir emphasized a bright overtone across the room. Rather than fix the masking, the group made it the basis of the arrangement.

Harmonium player and tape operator Mika Halme joined after building several unstable loops from the dome’s ventilation hum. Percussionist Eljas Niemi reduced his kit to one floor tom, one kick, one suspended cymbal, and heavy boots so rhythmic events could remain physical and rare.

Their first performances were called spectrum walks. Audience members stood along the circular coordinate markings while the singers and guitarist slowly changed position. Because the dome reflection altered with distance and angle, overtone balance shifted around the room without panning automation. No conventional stage was used; the architecture was treated as an instrument.

The scene developed a visual language of frost-white concrete, dull aluminum, navy wool, black amplifier cloth, faded orange coordinate paint, magnetic tape, pale breath vapor, work lamps, and deep winter blue. The unofficial rule is that no effect may create a texture that cannot still be traced back to breath, string, reed, membrane, room, or tape.

