Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Your Mouth Moves the Amplifier Snow
- Story & Context: Anu begins on one steady low fundamental while slowly moving a bright overtone upward through the harmonic series. Leena holds a bowed guitar drone through three distortion bands, but each time Anu’s overtone reaches a new spectral region, a narrow pocket opens in the guitar wall. Temir enters beneath them with a rough low drone a few cents below the guitar root, producing slow beating rather than a chord change. Mika records the first breath cycle to tape and lets it return unsynchronized every several seconds. Eljas waits nearly two minutes before one floor-tom strike tuned close to Temir’s fundamental. The middle section becomes denser without changing harmony: Anu sweeps faster, Leena widens the feedback field, and multiple tape returns begin phasing against the live room. The final minute removes guitar completely, revealing that the perceived “pad” still exists in the stacked voices, harmonium, and decaying dome reflection.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Frosted radio-dome interior seen through drifting breath vapor, a vocalist’s profile in close focus while amplifier speaker cloth behind appears to dissolve into white granular texture, magnetic tape crossing the floor like a dark contour line. No conventional lyrics; use original phonetic vowels, breath pulses, and sparse spoken fragments: “move the air,” “hold the root,” “snow inside the speaker,” “leave the overtone open.”

Track 2: Two Fundamentals Under One Roof
- Story & Context: Temir and Anu each choose a low fundamental separated by less than a semitone and sustain them in alternating breath cycles. The resulting difference tone becomes the piece’s hidden pulse. Leena does not play a chord progression; she bows one guitar string and slowly changes feedback position until amplifier overtones begin reinforcing and then cancelling parts of the vocal spectrum. Mika holds a harmonium fifth beneath the beating voices, giving the ear a stable anchor while the rest of the field seems to bend. Eljas places boot stamps only when the difference-tone pulse becomes strongest in the room, so rhythm appears to emerge from acoustics rather than from a metronome. Halfway through, one singer moves physically across the dome. The room reflection shifts, changing the apparent beating rate even though both sung fundamentals remain the same. The track ends with the two voices finally converging on unison while the tape loops continue to preserve the earlier disagreement.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Wide circular observatory floor with two vocalists standing on separate faded coordinate rings beneath one curved steel roof, low harmonium at center, guitar amplifier glowing dimly at the perimeter, breath clouds overlapping. Vocal material should be sustained vowels and overtone syllables with sparse original text: “two roots,” “one roof,” “closer without moving,” “the room keeps both.”

Track 3: Stop the Tape Before the Dome Stops Singing
- Story & Context: The EP closer begins with the largest accumulated tape field on the record: earlier breath sounds, harmonium reeds, amplifier hum, and room returns overlap in slow unstable loops. Live voices enter almost invisibly inside that archive, making it unclear which sounds belong to the present performance. Leena uses no distortion pedal at first, only E-bow and natural amplifier feedback. Over twelve minutes the process reverses. Mika stops one tape loop at a time, Eljas removes percussion, Leena closes each feedback path, and the singers reduce from layered overtones to plain fundamentals. The quieter the arrangement becomes, the more audible the observatory itself becomes: cable rattle, air movement, distant steel contraction. At the expected final swell, the band refuses to rebuild the wall. Mika stops the last reel. Both singers end together. The dome return continues several seconds longer than any performer, becoming the final “member” heard on the EP.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Reel-to-reel tape machine being stopped by hand in the foreground of a vast empty radio dome, two singers and a bowed guitarist barely visible in deep blue distance, last tape loop hanging slack, frost on steel ribs. Sparse original text only: “stop the memory,” “leave the roof,” “we are finished,” “the dome is not.”

